    ],
    tableHead: { col1: 'Category', col2: 'Cost', col3: 'Notes' },
    tip: 'April and May permits at USD 600 are genuinely excellent value. Trails are muddier but the gorillas are equally easy to find and tourist numbers are a fraction of peak season.',
  },
  {
    heading: 'What Does the Permit Include?',
    body: [
      'The USD 800 permit covers everything you need for the trek itself. It is not simply an entry ticket — it is a comprehensive activity package managed by the Uganda Wildlife Authority.',
    ],
    bullets: [
      'Bwindi Impenetrable Forest or Mgahinga Gorilla National Park entry fee',
      'Trained Uganda Wildlife Authority ranger guide throughout the trek',
      'Armed security escort through the forest',
      'Up to one hour of observation time with a habituated gorilla family',
      'A briefing by UWA staff before the trek begins',
      'A trekking certificate issued after the successful encounter',
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'How Far in Advance Should I Book?',
    body: [
      'This is the most common mistake travelers make: leaving permit booking too late. Bwindi has 19 habituated gorilla families approved for tourism, each visited by a maximum of 8 people per day. Total daily capacity is around 152 visitors — a small number given global demand.',
      'During the peak dry seasons (June to September and December to February) permits sell out 4 to 6 months in advance, sometimes longer for popular families like Mubare or Habinyanja. For Christmas and New Year dates, 8 to 10 months is not unusual.',
      'We strongly recommend booking your gorilla permit as the first step in planning your Uganda safari — then build the rest of the itinerary around your confirmed trek date.',
    ],
    bullets: [
      'Peak season (Jun to Sep, Dec to Feb): book 4 to 6 months ahead',
      'Christmas and New Year: book 8 to 10 months ahead',
      'Shoulder season (Oct to Nov): 2 to 3 months is usually sufficient',
      'Low season (Apr to May): sometimes available with 2 to 4 weeks notice',
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: 'Bwindi vs Mgahinga: Which Park?',
    body: [
      'Both Bwindi Impenetrable Forest and Mgahinga Gorilla National Park are in the same volcanic mountain region near the Rwanda border. Bwindi is by far the larger option with 19 habituated families across four trekking sectors: Buhoma, Rushaga, Nkuringo and Ruhija. Mgahinga has just one habituated family — the Nyakagezi group.',
      'For most visitors Bwindi is the clear choice because of the larger number of families and trekking sectors. Mgahinga is worth considering if you also want to see golden monkeys (the only park in Uganda where this is possible) or climb the Virunga volcanoes.',
